WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.137
The second set of gaps is
in the policy definition.

2
00:00:03.137 --> 00:00:07.503
The cmdlet accepts whatever identifier
and version you give it, so a fresh

3
00:00:07.503 --> 00:00:12.226
identifier quietly creates a second
definition instead of updating the first.

4
00:00:12.226 --> 00:00:17.462
An enforcing definition reaches every
machine in scope, whoever owns the setting.

5
00:00:17.462 --> 00:00:22.225
The switch that includes Arc-enabled servers
defaults to off, and nothing tells you.

6
00:00:22.225 --> 00:00:25.475
And Learn's storage token
example lasts three years.

7
00:00:25.475 --> 00:00:29.039
The verdict is simple: Learn's
procedure is the procedure,

8
00:00:29.039 --> 00:00:31.087
and the build replaces none of it.

9
00:00:31.087 --> 00:00:34.390
It cleans, checks and
records in between the steps.
